⏺ You're absolutely right on both counts! That's a really interesting use case - a Crank renderer for terminal UIs would be
  fantastic.

  Text Node Limitations

  Looking at the document model, it's very much widget-oriented. The basic hierarchy is:
  - Element → Text → Button/AnimatedText
  - Element → TextBox (which uses TextBuffer internally)

  But there's no simple "text node" primitive that just renders text inline within a container. Everything is either:
  1. A standalone Text element (positioned absolutely)
  2. Text content within other elements (like Button content)
  3. TextBox for multi-line text (but it's a full widget)

  For a React/Crank-like renderer, you'd want something like:
  <Container>
    Some text here
    <Button>Click me</Button>
    More text
  </Container>

  But terminal-kit doesn't really support that mixed content model.

  Layout is Declarative, Not Hierarchical

  Yeah, the Layout class takes a layout definition object rather than using the actual element hierarchy. Looking at the code, it
  seems like you pass a layout description and it positions elements accordingly, but it's not automatically derived from the
  component tree.

  For a Crank Renderer

  You'd probably need to:
  1. Create text node abstractions - maybe render text nodes as positioned Text elements or combine them into TextBox elements
  2. Build your own layout engine - translate JSX hierarchy into terminal-kit's positioning
  3. Handle reconciliation - map Crank's diffing to terminal-kit's element lifecycle

  It's definitely doable, but you'd be building a lot of the "web-like" abstractions that terminal-kit doesn't provide natively.
  The library is more designed for explicit, positioned UI construction rather than flow-based layouts.

