> Can we use HappyDOM but not use their css styling?


⏺ Absolutely! HappyDOM is modular enough that we can use just the DOM tree functionality and ignore their CSS engine entirely.

  Using HappyDOM Selectively

  import { Window } from 'happy-dom';

  class TOMDocument {
    private window: Window;
    private cssEngine: TOMStyleEngine; // Our own!

    constructor() {
      this.window = new Window();
      this.cssEngine = new TOMStyleEngine();

      // Override HappyDOM's CSS handling
      this.disableHappyDOMCSS();
      this.setupCustomStyling();
    }

    private disableHappyDOMCSS(): void {
      // Don't let HappyDOM process CSS
      const document = this.window.document;

      // Override style property to use our system
      Object.defineProperty(HTMLElement.prototype, 'style', {
        get() { return this._tomStyle || (this._tomStyle = {}); },
        set(value) { this._tomStyle = value; }
      });

      // Disable CSS parsing
      document.createElement = (tagName: string) => {
        const element = this.window.document.createElement.call(this, tagName);
        element._tomStyle = {}; // Our style object
        return element;
      };
    }

    private setupCustomStyling(): void {
      // Our simple styling system
      this.cssEngine.onStyleChange = (element, newStyle) => {
        // Trigger layout/render when styles change
        this.scheduleRender();
      };
    }
  }

  Custom Style System

  interface TOMStyle {
    // Layout
    display?: 'flex' | 'block' | 'none';
    flexDirection?: 'row' | 'column';
    justifyContent?: 'flex-start' | 'center' | 'flex-end';

    // Box model (character units)
    width?: number | string;
    height?: number | string;
    padding?: number;
    margin?: number;

    // Terminal styling
    color?: string;
    backgroundColor?: string;
    fontWeight?: 'normal' | 'bold';
    fontStyle?: 'normal' | 'italic';
  }

  class TOMStyleEngine {
    applyStyle(element: Element, style: TOMStyle): void {
      // Store style on element
      (element as any)._tomStyle = { ...style };

      // Apply to Yoga for layout
      this.applyToYoga(element, style);

      // Trigger re-render
      this.onStyleChange?.(element, style);
    }

    getComputedStyle(element: Element): TOMStyle {
      return (element as any)._tomStyle || {};
    }

    private applyToYoga(element: Element, style: TOMStyle): void {
      const yogaNode = this.getYogaNode(element);

      if (style.display) {
        yogaNode.setDisplay(this.mapDisplay(style.display));
      }
      if (style.flexDirection) {
        yogaNode.setFlexDirection(this.mapFlexDirection(style.flexDirection));
      }
      // etc...
    }
  }

  Clean Element Interface

  class TOMElement extends HTMLElement {
    private _tomStyle: TOMStyle = {};

    get style(): TOMStyle {
      return this._tomStyle;
    }

    set style(value: TOMStyle) {
      this._tomStyle = { ...value };
      tomStyleEngine.applyStyle(this, this._tomStyle);
    }

    // Override setAttribute to handle style changes
    setAttribute(name: string, value: string): void {
      super.setAttribute(name, value);

      if (name === 'class') {
        // Could apply class-based styles here
        this.applyClassStyles();
      }
    }
  }

  What We Get vs Skip

  ✅ Use from HappyDOM:

  - DOM tree structure (parent/child relationships)
  - Event system (addEventListener, bubbling)
  - Query selectors (querySelector, getElementById)
  - Node manipulation (appendChild, removeChild)
  - MutationObserver
  - TreeWalker, NodeIterator

  ❌ Skip from HappyDOM:

  - CSS parsing engine
  - CSS cascade/specificity
  - CSS selectors for styling (we still use them for querying)
  - CSSOM (CSSStyleSheet, etc.)
  - CSS animations/transitions
  - Media queries

  🔧 Replace with our own:

  - Simple style objects instead of CSS strings
  - Yoga for layout instead of CSS layout
  - Terminal-specific style properties
  - Bun.color for color processing

  Usage Example

  // HappyDOM handles the tree, we handle styling
  const document = new TOMDocument();

  const container = document.createElement('container');
  container.style = {  // Our simple style object
    display: 'flex',
    flexDirection: 'column',
    padding: 2,
    backgroundColor: 'blue'
