⏺ The problem with extending HappyDOM's Element class is that HappyDOM has a complex internal factory system and constructor
  requirements. Looking at the error:

  TypeError: Illegal constructor
  at new Node (/Users/briankim/Projects/terminal-kit/tom/node_modules/happy-dom/lib/nodes/node/Node.js:67:58)

  The issue is in this part of HappyDOM's Node constructor:

  // From HappyDOM's source
  if (!ownerDocument) {
      throw new TypeError('Illegal constructor');
  }

  HappyDOM Elements can't be constructed directly - they need to be created through the Document's factory methods. HappyDOM uses:

  1. Internal symbols for property access
  2. Document context that must be set during construction
  3. NodeFactory.pullOwnerDocument() to get the current document context
  4. Specific initialization order that we're breaking by calling super()

  When we try new TOMContainer() directly, there's no document context, so HappyDOM throws "Illegal constructor".

  The proper solutions are:

  1. Document.createElement() - Create elements through the document (what I was trying to fix)
  2. Composition over inheritance - Don't extend Element, just attach data to it
  3. Custom Elements API - Register proper custom elements with HappyDOM

  The composition approach I was implementing is actually cleaner because:
  - No fighting with HappyDOM's internal systems
  - We get all DOM functionality for free
  - Easier to test and debug
  - More flexible architecture

  Should I continue with the composition approach, or would you prefer to make the inheritance work by properly integrating with
  HappyDOM's factory system?

