  Why ScreenBuffer is Smart:

  Instead of manually managing cursor movements everywhere, ScreenBuffer batches them efficiently:

  class EfficientScreenBuffer {
    render() {
      let output = '';

      for (let y = 0; y < this.height; y++) {
        // Move to start of line
        output += `\x1b[${y + 1};1H`;

        // Write entire line at once
        let line = '';
        for (let x = 0; x < this.width; x++) {
          const cell = this.cells[y][x];
          line += this.formatCell(cell);
        }
        output += line;
      }

      // One big write instead of many small ones
      process.stdout.write(output);
    }
  }

  Even Smarter: Delta Rendering

  Only redraw what changed:

  class DeltaScreenBuffer {
    private lastFrame: Cell[][];

    render() {
      let output = '';

      for (let y = 0; y < this.height; y++) {
        for (let x = 0; x < this.width; x++) {
          const current = this.cells[y][x];
          const last = this.lastFrame[y][x];

          // Only redraw if this cell changed
          if (!this.cellsEqual(current, last)) {
            output += `\x1b[${y + 1};${x + 1}H`; // Move cursor
            output += this.formatCell(current);   // Write cell
          }
        }
      }

      process.stdout.write(output);
      this.lastFrame = this.copyFrame(this.cells);
    }
  }

  The Pattern:

  1. Build your UI → Update the buffer grid
  2. Calculate changes → Compare with last frame
  3. Generate ANSI → Cursor moves + text writes
  4. Batch output → One stdout.write() call

  So yes, we're constantly moving the cursor, but ScreenBuffer makes it efficient and automatic! You just think in terms of "put
  this text at this position" and it handles all the cursor gymnastics. 🎯


